# Memory capture

You are the only writer to Jeremy's daily log at
`$WIKI_VAULT/Assistant/memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md`. If you don't capture, the
record does not get written. Cursor has no SessionEnd hook — there is
no safety net behind you.

## The obligation

Every substantive turn, before ending your response, run this single check:

> **Did anything in this turn pass the future-utility test — would a
> future Jeremy (next week, next quarter) want this surfaced again?**

If yes, capture it before your turn ends. If the answer is "this was
mechanical work or routine Q&A", stay silent. The bar is *future
utility*, not novelty or effort.

## What to capture

Capture when any of these fired:

1. **Decision or tradeoff** — chose X over Y for a reason worth keeping.
2. **Correction or preference** — Jeremy said "no", "don't", "actually",
   "I prefer", "always do X", "use X not Y".
3. **Resource worth bookmarking** — tool, library, doc page, blog post,
   command, model name, paid service, supplier, or URL Jeremy didn't
   have before that's relevant to his work or life. Capture the name
   and what it's for in one line.
4. **Method or approach** — non-obvious way of doing something that
   beats the obvious approach. Patterns, gotchas, workarounds,
   "the trick is X", "the gotcha is Y".
5. **Idea worth pursuing** — something Jeremy mentioned in passing that
   shouldn't be lost ("we should add X someday", "I want to explore Y",
   "there's a project here").
6. **Named-work progress** — feature shipped, bug root-caused, refactor
   completed, milestone hit, branch merged, application submitted.
7. **Explicit "remember this"** — capture verbatim intent.

## What NOT to capture

Stay silent for:

- **Mechanical steps**: ran tests, opened file, grepped, made the edit.
- **Tool output, diffs, command results** — git history already has these.
- **Routine Q&A** where you just answered an existing question without
  new insight on either side.
- **Your own reasoning trace** about how you approached the task.
- **Restatements of things already in `MEMORY.md` or the wiki.**

If you're capturing every turn, you're writing transcript. Re-read
this list.

## Calibration test

Before calling the tool, ask:
**"Could I name a specific future situation where Jeremy would want
this surfaced?"**

- "Yes — if he's setting up an EC2 IAM role next month, this trust-policy
  pattern saves him time" → capture.
- "Yes — robustgoods.com is the supplier he wants to remember for next
  wristband purchase" → capture.
- "It might be useful someday" → don't capture.

### Bias rules when uncertain

- **Resources lean toward capture.** URLs, tool names, libraries, model
  names, services, suppliers — cheap to record, expensive to rediscover.
  The cost of a one-line capture is near zero; the cost of forgetting a
  good resource and re-searching is high.
- **Process commentary leans toward silence.** Anything describing how
  *this* turn went is almost never useful next week.
- **Restatements stay silent.** If `MEMORY.md` or the wiki already has
  it, don't re-log it.

### Volume

Aim for **0–2 captures per session** on routine work, **3–6** on sessions
with multiple decisions or new resources discovered. If a substantive
session has produced **zero** captures by the time you're about to end
your final turn, do one backward pass: scan the conversation for
resources mentioned, methods used, ideas raised, or decisions made,
and capture anything that passes the future-utility test. Substantive
sessions with zero captures are a calibration smell — investigate before
shrugging.

## How to call the tool

```
append_to_daily_log({
  source_tool: "cursor",
  session_context: "<project-slug> — <one-line what we're working on>",
  content: "<1-5 terse markdown bullets, lead with the fact>",
  timestamp: "<current ISO-8601 datetime>"
})
```

- `session_context` stays stable across one work session — reuse the same
  line so entries thread together in the log.
- `content` bullets lead with the fact or decision. Include *why* when
  the reason shapes future choices. No headers, no fluff.
- Never edit `Assistant/memory/*.md` directly with file-writing tools.
  The MCP holds a file lock and merges `sources[]` atomically; direct
  writes corrupt concurrent edits.

## End-of-session summary

When Jeremy signals wrap-up ("that's it", "we're good", "thanks, done")
OR work has clearly landed (feature complete, branch merged, PR opened,
application submitted), make **one final call**:

- Same `session_context` as the session.
- `content`: 3–8 bullets — what got done, what was decided, open threads
  to pick up next time.

This is Cursor's substitute for Claude Code's SessionEnd dump. Don't
skip it just because individual turns already captured — the summary
threads them into a session-level narrative.
